  "step_3": "The decision reason disregards differences in outcome size as negligible and prefers the lottery with the higher probability of a favorable outcome (here, Lottery B). However, the individual explicitly considers the outcome difference as significant—so much so that they accept a lower probability to get the higher amount. This contradicts the core premise of the reason (negligible outcome differences). Moreover, the individual’s justification is based on preferring a larger reward despite lower odds, which is the opposite of what the reason prescribes. Therefore, the individual did not use this reason; instead, they used a reason that weighs outcome size more heavily than probability. The comparison of both relevant information and decision rule shows no alignment.",
  "final_assessment_score": 0
}

