{
  "step_1": "The decision reason can be applied to the decision problem. The lotteries have distinct outcomes and probabilities. Lottery A offers 500 Euros with 100% probability, while Lottery B offers 1000 Euros with 50% probability and 0 Euros with 50% probability. The favorable outcome can be interpreted as receiving a non-zero amount. In Lottery A, the probability of a favorable outcome is 100% (since 500 Euros is guaranteed), whereas in Lottery B, it is 50% (only if 1000 Euros is realized). The reason would prefer Lottery A because it has a higher probability of a favorable outcome, assuming the difference between 500 and 1000 Euros is considered negligible. This assumption is critical, and the reason hinges on such a judgment of outcome similarity.",
